<<<
witch-. See wych-.
===
**witch-.** See wych-.
>>>

<<<
witenagemot. Pron. wi’tenagimd’t.
===
**witenagemot.** Pron. wĭ'tenagĭmō't.
>>>

<<<
with. Writers who have become conscious
===
**with.** Writers who have become conscious
>>>

<<<
of as to & in the CASE of, casting about for a substitute
===
of ^^AS^^ *to* & *in the* ^^CASE^^ *of*, casting about for a substitute
>>>

<<<
have lately hit upon with, which is sometimes found
===
have lately hit upon *with*, which is sometimes found
>>>

<<<
found displacing of or some really appropriate
===
found displacing *of* or some really appropriate
>>>

<<<
avoided:—Wiih pipes, as with tobacco, William Bragge was one of the most successful collectors./
===
avoided:—*With pipes, as with tobacco, William Bragge was one of the most successful collectors.*/
>>>

<<<
Collins has had his excellent editors, & we must suppose that the manuscript has finally disappeared; but, with the others, we suspect that the poems are exiant.
===
*Collins has had his excellent editors, & we must suppose that the manuscript has finally disappeared; but, with the others, we suspect that the poems are extant.*
>>>

<<<
extant.*/Read of pipes, of tobacco, the poems of the others.
===
extant.*/Read *of pipes, of tobacco, the poems of the others.*
>>>

<<<
withal. See Warpour STREET.
===
**withal.** See ^^WARDOUR STREET^^.
>>>

<<<
withe, withy. Both spellings, & the monosyllabic as well as the disyllabic pronunciation, are in use.
===
**withe, withy.** Both spellings, & the monosyllabic as well as the disyllabic pronunciation, are in use.
>>>

<<<
there is the plural withs in the A. V. of Judg: xvi. 7.
===
there is the plural *withs* in the A. V. of *Judg.* xvi. 7.
>>>

<<<
But probably withy, pl. -ies, is the best form for modern purposes, obviating uncertainty.
===
But probably *withy*, pl. *-ies*, is the best form for modern purposes, obviating uncertainty.
>>>

<<<
without. 1. W.=outside. 2. W.= unless. 3. Without or without. 4. Without hardly. 5. Without him being. 6. Negative confusion.
===
**without.** 1. *W.*=outside. 2. *W.*=unless. 3. *Without or without.* 4. *Without hardly.* 5. *Without him being.* 6. Negative confusion.
>>>

<<<
1. W.=outside. Both as adverb (listening to the wind without; clean within & without), & as preposition (is without the pale of civilization), the word retains this meaning;
===
**1.** *W.*=outside. Both as adverb *(listening to the wind without; clean within & without)*, & as preposition *(is without the pale of civilization)*, the word retains this meaning;
>>>

<<<
may be very incongruous. 2. W.=unless. No high efficiency can be secured without we first secure the hearty cooperation of the 30,000,000 or so workers.
===
may be very incongruous.

**2.** *W.*=unless. *No high efficiency can be secured* without we first secure *the hearty cooperation of the 30,000,000 or so workers.*
>>>

<<<
3. Without... or without... It can be done without any fear of his knowing it, or without other evil consequences. The well meant repetition of without is not merely needless, but wrong. See or 4.
===
**3.** *Without . . . or without . . . It can be done without any fear of his knowing it,* or without *other evil consequences.* The well meant repetition of *without* is not merely needless, but wrong. See ^^OR^^ 4.
>>>

<<<
4. Without hardly. The introduction of the vast new refineries has been brought about quickly, silently, & effectively, & without the surrounding community hardly being aware | of what was happening.
===
**4.** *Without hardly.* *The introduction of the vast new refineries has been brought about quickly, silently, & effectively,* & without the surrounding community hardly being aware *of what was happening.*
>>>

<<<
Again, like 2, a common colloquialism, but, unlike it, one that should never appear outside spoken or printed | talk; the English for without hardly — is almost without.
===
Again, like 2, a common colloquialism, but, unlike it, one that should never appear outside spoken or printed talk; the English for *without hardly* is almost *without.*
>>>

<<<
5. Without him being. The word is © peculiarly apt to usher in a FusEpD © PARTICIPLE, e.g. The formidable occasion had come & gone without anything dreadful happening.
===
**5.** *Without him being.* The word is peculiarly apt to usher in a ^^FUSED PARTICIPLE^^, e.g. *The formidable occasion had come & gone without anything dreadful happening.*
>>>

<<<
The fused participle is no worse after without than elsewhere, but those who are prepared to eschew it altogether should take warning that without will sometimes try their — virtue, so often does the temptation present itself;
===
The fused participle is no worse after *without* than elsewhere, but those who are prepared to eschew it altogether should take warning that *without* will sometimes try their virtue, so often does the temptation present itself;
>>>

<<<
it is, for instance, a pure accident that the sentence quoted in 4 for a different point contains the fused participle without the community being aware.
===
it is, for instance, a pure accident that the sentence quoted in 4 for a different point contains the fused participle *without the community being aware.*
>>>

<<<
Escapes | are usually not hard to find; here ‘ & nothing dreadful had happened °’, © or ‘ without any dreadful results °, would do, but particular suggestions for a particular case are of little value;
===
Escapes are usually not hard to find; here ' & nothing dreadful had happened ', or ' without any dreadful results ', would do, but particular suggestions for a particular case are of little value;
>>>

<<<
6. Negative confusion. Like all negative & virtually negative words, without often figures in such absurdities as:—It is not safe for any young lady to walk along the Spantards-road on a Sunday evening by herself
===
**6.** Negative confusion. Like all negative & virtually negative words, *without* often figures in such absurdities as:—*It is* not safe *for any young lady to walk along the Spaniards-road on a Sunday evening by herself*
>>>

<<<
without having unpleasant remarks spoken as she passes along./ Rendering it possible for a Government to accept some at any rate of the recommendations of the Committee
===
without having *unpleasant remarks spoken as she passes along.*/*Rendering it possible for a Government to accept some at any rate of the recommendations of the Committee*
>>>
